es\": {\n   \"default\": [],\n   \"items\": {\n    \"type\": \"string\"\n   },\n   \"title\": \"Semantic Changes\",\n   \"type\": \"array\"\n  },\n  \"requirement\": {\n   \"anyOf\": [\n    {\n     \"type\": \"string\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"type\": \"null\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"default\": null,\n   \"title\": \"Requirement\"\n  },\n  \"responsibility\": {\n   \"default\": \"\",\n   \"title\": \"Responsibility\",\n   \"type\": \"string\"\n  },\n  \"prerequisites\": {\n   \"default\": [],\n   \"items\": {\n    \"$ref\": \"#/$defs/EventRequirement\"\n   },\n   \"title\": \"Prerequisites\",\n   \"type\": \"array\"\n  },\n  \"comparisons\": {\n   \"default\": [],\n   \"items\": {\n    \"$ref\": \"#/$defs/EventComparison\"\n   },\n   \"title\": \"Comparisons\",\n   \"type\": \"array\"\n  },\n  \"knowledge\": {\n   \"default\": [],\n   \"items\": {\n    \"$ref\": \"#/$defs/KnowledgeUse\"\n   },\n   \"title\": \"Knowledge\",\n   \"type\": \"array\"\n  },\n  \"previous_id\": {\n   \"anyOf\": [\n    {\n     \"type\": \"string\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"type\": \"null\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"default\": null,\n   \"title\": \"Previous Id\"\n  },\n  \"revision_reason\": {\n   \"default\": \"\",\n   \"title\": \"Revision Reason\",\n   \"type\": \"string\"\n  }\n },\n \"required\": [\n  \"investigation_id\",\n  \"harness_path\",\n  \"source_ids\",\n  \"scope\",\n  \"prefix\"\n ],\n \"title\": \"ExperimentRequest\",\n \"type\": \"object\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_27","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-17_17-50-22-hashicorp_raft-real-run && /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -c \\\"\nimport json\ns=json.load(open('state.json'))\nfor i in s['interpretations']:\n    if i['id'].startswith('863168944c24456abb8baac28b498c39'):\n        print(i['id'])\n        print('EXECS', json.dumps(i.get('executions')))\n        print('COUNTER', json.dumps(i.get('counterevidence'),indent=1))\n        print('LIMITS', json.dumps(i.get('limitations'),indent=1))\n\\\"\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_27","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-17_17-50-22-hashicorp_raft-real-run && /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -c \\\"\nimport json\ns=json.load(open('state.json'))\nfor i in s['interpretations']:\n    if i['id'].startswith('863168944c24456abb8baac28b498c39'):\n        print(i['id'])\n        print('EXECS', json.dumps(i.get('executions')))\n        print('COUNTER', json.dumps(i.get('counterevidence'),indent=1))\n        print('LIMITS', json.dumps(i.get('limitations'),indent=1))\n\\\"\"","aggregated_output":"863168944c24456abb8baac28b498c39-interpretation-0\nEXECS {\"334d7a88f3b244548f316bcdb42b779e\": [0, 1]}\nCOUNTER [\n \"The documentation's promise is soft rather than a guarantee: it says it is 'very likely' the target wins and that a follower which cannot be promoted 'will fail gracefully' (api.go:1296-1330), and the leaderLoop code deliberately reports nil with the message 'lost leadership during transfer (expected)', which can be read as the future reporting only that leadership was relinquished. The claim therefore rests on the admission of an ineligible target and on the observed outcome, not on the future's wording alone.\",\n \"The control cases show the same mechanism transferring leadership correctly: the explicit transfer to an ordinary voter installed that named voter with a single term advance, and the later automatic control completed in about a hundred milliseconds with leadership on a voter and no leaderless samples. The defect is therefore specific to the eligibility of an explicitly named target, not to leadership transfer in general or to the entry-point harness's sequencing.\",\n \"The measured disruption is small in absolute terms because the cluster ran with inmemConfig timeouts (Heartbeat/Election/Lease 50 ms): the three trigger runs recorded 8, 7 and 6 leaderless samples (about 80, 70 and 60 ms) and one extra election round, against zero leaderless samples in the voter controls. The interval follows the configured election timeout, so a production configuration of about 1 s would scale it accordingly; these millisecond figures are not a bound for other settings.\",\n \"No committed entry was lost, no vote was counted for an ineligible candidate, and no decision-safety property was contradicted in either case; what is contradicted is the completion signal and the authority outcome of the explicit transfer.\",\n \"Both corrected runs show the target's own configuration had already converged to non-voter before the request, so the exercised case is a member that knew it could not win; a demoted member whose own view still lagged (a stale configuration) was not exercised, although the earliest runs exercised an unknown mix.\",\n \"The demotion race is a negative result for elevation: in 20 attempts issued back to back, no target became leader and every transfer reported success, so the timing gap that could have produced a worse outcome did not do so in this harness.\",\n \"The future's result is not uniform across receipts: the demotion race receipt 62e8b8bdcc9f448687b6588dda84125d recorded successful transfer futures in all twenty attempts, while receipt a319426e86514f45b2053a7b3bd8050b recorded 'leadership transfer in progress' on every one of five attempts on a four-member cluster whose demoted member's view had already converged. The sentence that the future completes without error therefore describes those receipts and not the operation in general, and the durable observation common to both is that the named member is never installed and no suffrage check refuses it. The repeat of the entry-point comparison, receipt e023eecf02c74f4db5bc8c5a16fd3586, reproduced the in-progress surface exactly, with the same error on all five attempts over 1036 ms, so within that harness the surface is stable. A second run of the demotion race, receipt 334d7a88f3b244548f316bcdb42b779e, reproduced that surface, with twenty successful futures and no attempt in which the named member led.\",\n \"The in-progress error seen in receipt a319426e86514f45b2053a7b3bd8050b is not reproduced here: with the transfer flag clear before the call, the same explicit request returned no error at all. The account therefore cannot claim that the error is a stable consequence of naming a non-voter, only that neither outcome is a refusal keyed to the target's suffrage and neither installs the target.\"\n]\nLIMITS [\n \"The cluster size is four members throughout, with three voters after the demotion, and the transfer always targets a non-leader member. The demotion race covers a transfer issued concurrently with the demotion, but a cluster whose only non-voter is the sole reachable member and a transfer that lands while the leader is already losing office to another election were not tested.\",\n \"Leaderless counts are sampled every ~10 ms over roughly 4.7 s of the 5 s window (470 to 471 samples); a gap shorter than the sample spacing would be missed, so 60 to 80 ms is a lower bound for the trigger and the controls' zero does not prove uninterrupted leadership.\",\n \"Only immediate convergence was observed in the corrected runs: the demoted member's own view reported the new role within the first poll (0 ms), so no single-case run exercised a target whose configuration still showed it as a voter at request time. The demotion race issues the transfer before applying, but it records the target's final role rather than its view at request time.\",\n \"That the voter members had committed the demotion before the transfer is inferred from their rejection and term adoption, not from reading each voter's configuration.\",\n \"Out of scope: whether an unprivileged or misconfigured peer could send TimeoutNow directly, even though timeoutNow performs no term, leader or membership check (raft.go:2232-2237); that path was not exercised here.\",\n \"Three harnesses now give two reproduced and one single futures outcome for the same request: the demotion race returned successful futures in all twenty attempts on both of its runs, the entry-point harness returned 'leadership transfer in progress' on all five attempts on both of its runs, and the flag probe returned no error on its single run with the transfer flag clear. The difference between the flag probe and the other two therefore lies in their preceding steps, which include repeated leader and configuration polling before the transfer, and which of those steps sets the transfer state is not yet identified; all three keep the demoted member's own view reporting the non-voter role before the call.\"\n]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
