{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_37","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"No experiment had been executed since the last update, so I carried forward the prepared flag-window repeat for 2690c8dadb9c4631b3fe90d9626cadb9-experiment and revalidated it against the submission schema. Repeating that fixed harness from its retained inputs gives the third transfer surface a second run: the demotion race and the entry-point comparison already report stable futures outcomes on two runs each, while the flag probe has only one, where the transfer flag read false before the call, the single explicit attempt returned no error in 0 ms, and the named demoted member never led. A second run either confirms that the nil future is a stable property of the flag-clear path or, if it returns 'leadership transfer in progress', shows the two surfaces vary run to run, which would force the account to drop the reading that the flag-clear path differs systematically from the polling harnesses. Either outcome directly addresses the recorded limitation that which preceding step sets the transfer state is still unidentified, and the flag readings before, during and after the call make the difference attributable rather than merely observed.\"}"}}
